Runtime packaging layer — how NSTT actually ships
Here’s the minimal, practical packaging stack for your three deployment profiles.

1. Local single‑node (dev/lab)
Profile: Red Bird lab mode.

Packaging:

Node.js app: Orchestrator + Core Six in one process

Electron shell (optional): for a desktop UI around the Governance Dashboard

Artifacts:

nstt-orchestrator-local (Node project)

nstt-core-six (shared library with agents, harness, router, dashboard, sim)

2. Distributed multi‑node (containers)
Profile: Merlin Dev governance mode.

Packaging:

Docker images per agent:

nstt-agent-red-bird

nstt-agent-merlin-dev

nstt-agent-oracle

nstt-agent-sentinel

nstt-agent-archivist

nstt-agent-fabricator

Orchestrator container: nstt-orchestrator-distributed

Communication:

gRPC/WebSocket/HTTP between Orchestrator and agents

Deployment:

Compose/Kubernetes for multi‑agent clusters

3. Cross‑platform mesh (prod)
Profile: NSTT production mode.

Packaging:

Backend: same Dockerized agents + orchestrator

Desktop: Electron app talking to orchestrator API

Browser: extension or web client calling orchestrator

Mobile: thin clients (React Native/Flutter/etc.) calling orchestrator

Governance:

Orchestrator runs in a hardened backend

Dashboard as a web UI backed by GovernanceDashboard logs

Minimal packaging structure
/core — shared TypeScript library (RuneEngine, HandshakeCoordinator, SchemaValidator, NSTTAgent, CoreSixHarness, Orchestrator)

/local-app — Node/Electron app for single‑node dev

/agents/* — per‑agent services (each with a small HTTP/gRPC wrapper around NSTTAgent)

/orchestrator-service — HTTP/gRPC API exposing Orchestrator

/dashboard-ui — web/Electron UI reading from GovernanceDashboard logs

/sim-runner — CLI/CI tool that runs SimulationEngine suites

If you want, next we can sketch the cross‑platform agent communication protocol (message schema, auth, rune‑aware headers) so these containers and clients talk in a governance‑consistent way.



1. Core runtime library (@nstt/core)
This is your shared TypeScript package that everything else depends on.

Contains:

RuneEngine (opcodes, execution)

HandshakeCoordinator (zero‑trust agent handshake)

SchemaValidator (audit‑grade validation)

NSTTAgent (governed agent primitive)

CoreSixHarness (Red Bird, Merlin Dev, Oracle, Sentinel, Archivist, Fabricator)

ConstellationRouter (task routing)

GovernanceDashboard (trace + violations)

SimulationEngine (stress‑tests)

NSTTOrchestrator (control plane)

This compiles to ESM + CJS so Node, Electron, and browser builds can all use it.

2. Backend runtime (Node + containers)
Goal: Run Orchestrator + agents as services.

Orchestrator service:

Node server exposing:

POST /execute → routed single‑agent task

POST /execute-governed → full Core Six run

POST /simulate → run a simulation scenario

Internally uses NSTTOrchestrator from @nstt/core.

Agent services:

Each agent (Red Bird, Merlin Dev, Oracle, etc.) can be:

In‑process (local profile), or

Separate container (distributed/mesh profile)

Each exposes a simple API:

POST /process → wraps NSTTAgent.process()

Packaging:

Dockerfiles per service

Orchestrator + agents orchestrated via Compose/Kubernetes.

3. Desktop runtime (Electron)
Goal: Give you a governance console on your machine.

Main process:

Embeds Orchestrator (local profile) directly via @nstt/core.

Renderer:

UI for:

Rune timelines (from GovernanceDashboard.snapshot)

Violation lists

Simulation controls (run scenarios, view results)

Electron just becomes a shell around the same core runtime.

4. Browser runtime (web client / extension)
Goal: Let browser clients talk to the NSTT Orchestrator.

Web client:

Calls backend Orchestrator API:

/execute for single tasks

/execute-governed for full multi‑agent runs

Displays:

Output

Governance snapshot (rune trace + violations)

Extension (optional):

Injects a UI into pages

Sends tasks to Orchestrator

Receives governed responses.

5. Mobile runtime (thin clients)
Goal: Mobile apps that use the same governed constellation.

Clients:

React Native / Flutter / native

Call Orchestrator API

Show:

Output

Basic governance status (valid/invalid, violation count)

All governance logic stays server‑side in the Orchestrator; mobile stays thin.

6. Cross‑platform runtime glue
Across all platforms, the pattern is:

One core library (@nstt/core) with:

agents, harness, router, dashboard, sim, orchestrator.

Multiple shells:

Node/containers (backend)

Electron (desktop)

Web client/extension (browser)

Mobile apps (clients)
